# Shell scripts keep Unix line endings on every checkout, including a Windows
# one.
#
# Git for Windows converts LF to CRLF by default, and a shell script with
# carriage returns in it does not run: dash reports `Syntax error: word
# unexpected` on the first `case`, because the `\r` is part of the word.  The
# CI job on windows-latest found exactly that.  These scripts are not run on
# Windows, but the repository is checked out there, and a file that is
# corrupt on disk is worth preventing whether or not anything reads it today.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The PowerShell scripts are deliberately left alone. Every PowerShell that
# will read them is happy with either ending, and pinning them to CRLF would
# rewrite the working copy on this machine for no benefit.
